{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Auburn and Syracuse Railroad -- Auburn's link to the Erie Canal at Syracuse, and one of the eight upstate New York roads that became the New York Central. Incorporated May 1 1834 with $400,000 of capital, organized Jan 20 1835, and located Aug 22 1835 under chief engineer Edwin F. Johnson. Construction began in 1835 and was nearly halted by the Panic of 1837; a $200,000 state loan (act of Apr 18 1838) and six-month interest-bearing company notes carried it through. Built on the cheap with wooden stringers and oak battens instead of iron rail, it opened Jan 8 1838 with horse-drawn trains running only as far as Geddes, and went over to steam in 1839, when a trestle across the Syracuse millpond finally carried the line into Vanderbilt Square. The company deliberately served the Erie Canal rather than competing with it, paying the state the canal freight toll in exchange for the unusual right to haul freight as well as passengers. Standard gauge from the outset, since it was meant as a link in the Albany-Buffalo chain. On Aug 1 1850 it merged with the Auburn and Rochester to form the Rochester and Syracuse Railroad, which passed to the New York Central in 1853; the Rochester and Syracuse Direct line opened the same year and demoted the 'Auburn Road' to a secondary route. NYC passenger service over it ended in 1958. The corridor survives in freight use as the Finger Lakes Railway, which took it over from Conrail in 1995. Geometry covers only the original A&S, Auburn to Syracuse.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auburn_and_Syracuse_Railroad (incorporated May 1 1834; construction begun 1835, delayed by the Panic of 1837, completed by January 1838; opened for business Jan 8 1838 with horse-drawn trains; first locomotive 'Syracuse' introduced June 14 1839; 26-mile run in 58 minutes in late 1839; freight allowed under the canal-protection laws; merged Aug 1 1850 into the Rochester and Syracuse Railroad; part of the New York Central in 1853; at least two passenger trains per day each way into the 1940s and one a day until 1958; last-years station list)", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Auburn_and_Syracuse_Railroad.map (CC BY 4.0) -- digitized right-of-way geometry used for all four features here", "http://www.crookedlakereview.com/articles/67_100/94jan1996/94palmer.html -- Richard F. Palmer, 'The Auburn Road: A Study in Early Engineering', Crooked Lake Review, January 1996 (incorporation May 1 1834 with $400,000 capital, aided by State Senator William H. Seward; organized Jan 20 1835; Edwin F. Johnson chief engineer; route located Aug 22 1835; standard gauge 4 ft 8.75 in; 25.73 miles Auburn depot to Syracuse depot, 271 ft total descent, 62 percent tangent, maximum grade 30 ft per mile; Nine Mile Creek valley embankment and the two-mile rock cut at Camillus; wooden stringers with oak battens; $200,000 state loan act of Apr 18 1838; Dec 25 1837 trial run averaging 10 mph; first official trip to Geddes Jan 8 1838, Auburn 9:30 am - Geddes 12:30 pm; Sherwood stagecoach operating contract; Buckingham's Aug 9 1838 account of four hours for 22 miles; inaugural steam trip June 4 1839 with the Rogers, Ketchum and Grosvenor 4-2-0s 'Syracuse' and 'Auburn'; Garden Street freight spur abandoned 1841; Finger Lakes Railway took the line over from Conrail in 1995)", "http://streamlinermemories.info/NYC/NYC48-4TT.pdf -- New York Central timetable, Apr 25 1948, Table 51 (at least two passenger trains each way daily, plus a Geneva-Syracuse train daily except Sundays)", "https://www.crookedlakereview.com/articles/136_167/137fall2005/137palmer3.htm -- Richard Palmer, 'The Finger Lakes Railway', Crooked Lake Review, Fall 2005 (the New York Central operated passenger trains on the Auburn Road until May 18 1958; abandoned Victor-Pittsford 1960, Canandaigua-Victor 1978, Pittsford-Rochester 1982; Penn Central 1967-1976, Conrail Apr 1 1976 to Jul 1 1995, then the county-owned Finger Lakes Railway from Jul 23 1995)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Headways in every segment are estimates: no timetable has been consulted for the horse-drawn, A&S steam, Rochester and Syracuse, or 1853-1948 New York Central eras, and the 1948-1958 figure is derived from a train count rather than a printed headway. Wikipedia dates the first steam locomotive to June 14 1839 while Palmer, citing the Auburn Gazette of June 5 1839, puts the inaugural steam trip on June 4 -- the discrepancy is unresolved and the segment boundary simply uses 1839. Running times before 1948 are unsourced. The geometry is the main line only: the 1838-1839 temporary Geddes terminus, the Garden Street freight spur down to the Owasco Outlet mills in Auburn (abandoned 1841), and the successive Syracuse depot sites are not depicted separately.", "horsecar_audit": "2026-08-24: removed as part of a repo-wide horsecar audit. Removed here: whole feature auburn-syracuse-railroad-horsedrawn (whole feature; the A&S opened Jan 8 1838 with horse-drawn trains and did not use steam until 1839). REPO RULE 5: this map shows MECHANICALLY powered rail -- horsecars and mule cars are out of scope, and a line's drawn years must start when it stopped using animals. Notes may still mention the animal era as context. See CLAUDE.md.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Auburn and Syracuse Railroad", "line": "Auburn and Syracuse: Auburn \u2013 Syracuse", "id": "auburn-syracuse-railroad-steam", "color": "#8E3B46", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: 10.5-ton 4-2-0 locomotives on strap iron rail; a record run in late 1839 covered the 26 miles in 58 minutes (~43 km/h), well above ordinary scheduled speed with stops", "segments": [{"start": 1839, "end": 1850, "headway_min": 360, "note": "First steam operation in 1839 with the Rogers, Ketchum and Grosvenor 4-2-0s 'Syracuse' and 'Auburn'; the battens were progressively replaced with 2.5 by 0.75 inch strap iron. The trestle across the Syracuse millpond was finished in early 1839 and trains ran through to Vanderbilt Square, sharing the new Syracuse and Utica Railroad station. Known locally as the 'Auburn Road'. Frequency estimated/unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auburn_and_Syracuse_Railroad", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Auburn_and_Syracuse_Railroad.map", "http://www.crookedlakereview.com/articles/67_100/94jan1996/94palmer.html", "http://streamlinermemories.info/NYC/NYC48-4TT.pdf", "https://www.crookedlakereview.com/articles/136_167/137fall2005/137palmer3.htm"]}], "geometry_source": "Digitized right-of-way from Wikimedia Commons (Data:Auburn and Syracuse Railroad.map, CC BY 4.0), 408 points, Auburn to downtown Syracuse. Measured length 41.75 km / 25.94 mi, matching Edwin F. Johnson's 1837 figure of 25.73 miles from the Auburn depot to the contemplated Syracuse depot. Not a waypoint approximation."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-76.57032, 42.93464], [-76.57021, 42.93485], [-76.57012, 42.93502], [-76.57008, 42.93528], [-76.57006, 42.93542], [-76.57004, 42.93622], [-76.57001, 42.93685], [-76.56993, 42.93769], [-76.5698, 42.93893], [-76.56968, 42.94008], [-76.56965, 42.94031], [-76.56957, 42.94104], [-76.56947, 42.9